{"graph_chars": "The exam trap named Lee. A drought hits wheat. Lee correctly shifts supply left: lower quantity, higher price. Then Lee says the higher price makes farmers supply more, so supply shifts right again, and the higher price also cuts demand so demand shifts left. Wrong. A shift in one curve never causes a shift in the other. It causes a movement along the other curve. A change in this good's own price never shifts its demand or supply curve. Keep cause and effect in order. In real markets the jump to a new equilibrium is not instant. Prices and quantities head that way, and another shock often arrives before they arrive.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0617], [0.0617, 0.1235], [0.1235, 0.1852], [0.1852, 0.2469], [0.2469, 0.3087], [0.3087, 0.3704], [0.3704, 0.4321], [0.4321, 0.4938], [0.4938, 0.5556], [0.5556, 0.6173], [0.6173, 0.679], [0.679, 0.7408], [0.7408, 0.8025], [0.8025, 0.8642], [0.8642, 0.926], [0.926, 0.9877], [0.9877, 1.0494], [1.0494, 1.1112], [1.1112, 1.1729], [1.1729, 1.2346], [1.2346, 1.2963], [1.2963, 1.3581], [1.3581, 1.4198], [1.4198, 1.4815], [1.4815, 1.5433], [1.5433, 1.605], [1.605, 1.6667], [1.6667, 1.7285], [1.7285, 1.7902], [1.7902, 1.8519], [1.8519, 1.9137], [1.9137, 1.9754], [1.9754, 2.0371], [2.0371, 2.0988], [2.0988, 2.1606], [2.1606, 2.2223], [2.2223, 2.284], [2.284, 2.3458], [2.3458, 2.4075], [2.4075, 2.4692], [2.4692, 2.531], [2.531, 2.5927], [2.5927, 2.6544], [2.6544, 2.7162], [2.7162, 2.7779], [2.7779, 2.8396], [2.8396, 2.9013], [2.9013, 2.9631], [2.9631, 3.0248], [3.0248, 3.0865], [3.0865, 3.1483], [3.1483, 3.21], [3.21, 3.2717], [3.2717, 3.3335], [3.3335, 3.3952], [3.3952, 3.4569], [3.4569, 3.5187], [3.5187, 3.5804], [3.5804, 3.6421], [3.6421, 3.7038], [3.7038, 3.7656], [3.7656, 3.8273], [3.8273, 3.889], [3.889, 3.9508], [3.9508, 4.0125], [4.0125, 4.0742], [4.0742, 4.136], [4.136, 4.1977], [4.1977, 4.2594], [4.2594, 4.3212], [4.3212, 4.3829], [4.3829, 4.4446], [4.4446, 4.5063], [4.5063, 4.5681], [4.5681, 4.6298], [4.6298, 4.